mstead"],["Dagwood sandwich","dish/dagwood-sandwich"],["Deep-dish pizza","dish/deep-dish-pizza"],["Desperation pie","dish/desperation-pie"],["Dirty rice","dish/dirty-rice"],["Donut hole","dish/donut-hole"],["Egg in the basket","dish/egg-in-the-basket"],["Fish and chips","dish/fish-and-chips"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/ff/Frysauce.JPG/500px-Frysauce.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Authalic","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frysauce.JPG"}},"ingredient/thai-basil":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/thai-basil","n":"Thai basil","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Thai basil is a type of basil native to and widely used throughout Southeast Asia, that has been cultivated to provide distinctive traits. Its flavor is described as anise- and licorice-like and slightly spicy. It is more stable under high or extended cooking temperatures than sweet basil. Thai basil has small, narrow leaves, purple stems, and pink-purple flowers."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-thai-basil"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thai_basil"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Thai cuisine","cuisine/thai-cuisine",[["Green curry","dish/green-curry"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Curry","ingredient/curry"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Kaffir lime","ingredient/kaffir-lime"],["Palm sugar","ingredient/palm-sugar"],["Thai eggplant","ingredient/thai-eggplant"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/1f/Thai_basil.jpg/500px-Thai_basil.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Unknown author","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thai_basil.jpg"}},"ingredient/salmon-as-food":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/salmon-as-food","n":"Salmon as food","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["Salmon is a common food fish classified as an oily fish with a rich content of protein and omega-3 fatty acids. Norway is a major producer of farmed and wild salmon, accounting for more than 50% of global salmon production. Farmed and wild salmon differ only slightly in terms of food quality and safety, with farmed salmon having lower content of environmental contaminants, and wild salmon having higher content of omega-3 fatty acids."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-salmon-as-food"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salmon_as_food"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine",[["Lohikeitto","dish/lohikeitto"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Leek","ingredient/leek"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/4e/Sashimi_-_Maguro_Restaurant%2C_Bangna%2C_Bangkok_%2844856596864%29.jpg/500px-Sashimi_-_Maguro_Restaurant%2C_Bangna%2C_Bangkok_%2844856596864%29.jpg","w":500,"h":333,"by":"Vee Satayamas from Bangkok, Thailand","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sashimi_-_Maguro_Restaurant%2C_Bangna%2C_Bangkok_(44856596864).jpg"}},"dish/paloc-soup":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/paloc-soup","n":"Palóc soup","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Hungarian cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Palóc soup is a soup similar to Hungarian goulash soup, but lighter and a bit sour in taste. Contrary to popular opinion, the soup was not named after the Palóc people."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-paloc-soup"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pal%C3%B3c_soup"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Hungarian cuisine","cuisine/hungarian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":5,"links":[["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Green bean","ingredient/green-bean"],["Lard","ingredient/lard"],["Parsley","ingredient/parsley"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Zsuzsa Is in the Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"zsuzsaisinthekitchen.blogspot.com","note":"Hungarian-born home cook's blog of traditional Hungarian recipes.","url":"https://zsuzsaisinthekitchen.blogspot.com/2010/01","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Hungarian cuisine","links":[["Arany galuska","dish/arany-galuska"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chicken paprikash","dish/chicken-paprikash"],["Esterházy torte","dish/esterhazy-torte"],["Goulash","dish/goulash"],["Gulyásleves","dish/gulyasleves"],["Jókai bean soup","dish/jokai-bean-soup"],["Kürtőskalács","dish/kurtoskalacs"],["Lecsó","dish/lecso"],["Liptauer","dish/liptauer"],["Pörkölt","dish/porkolt"],["Rakott krumpli","dish/rakott-krumpli"],["Rigójancsi","dish/rigojancsi"],["Sour cherry soup","dish/sour-cherry-soup"],["Speckkuchen","dish/speckkuchen"],["Székelykáposzta","dish/szekelykaposzta"],["Tarhonya","dish/tarhonya"],["Trdelník","dish/trdelnik"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/27/Pal%C3%B3cleves_-_Gundel.jpg/500px-Pal%C3%B3cleves_-_Gundel.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"dpotera","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pal%C3%B3cleves_-_Gundel.jpg"}},"ingredient/white-chocolate":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/white-chocolate","n":"White chocolate","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["White chocolate is chocolate made from cocoa butter, sugar, and milk solids. It is ivory in color and lacks the dark appearance of most other types of chocolate because it does not contain the non-fat solid components of cocoa (cocoa powder). Due to this omission, as well as its sweetness and the occasional use of additives, some consumers do not consider white chocolate to be real chocolate."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-white-chocolate"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_chocolate"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Peppermint bark","dish/peppermint-bark"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Candy cane","ingredient/candy-cane"],["Caramel","ingredient/caramel"],["Dark chocolate","ingredient/dark-chocolate"],["Peppermint","ingredient/peppermint"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/4/47/Chopped_white_chocolate_chunks.jpg/500px-Chopped_white_chocolate_chunks.jpg","w":500,"h":524,"by":"Mixy Lorenzo","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chopped_white_chocolate_chunks.jpg"}},"dish/pincho":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/pincho","n":"Pincho","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["A pincho, pintxo (Basque: [pintʃo]) or pinchu (Asturian: [ˈpintʃʊ]) is a small snack, typically eaten in bars or taverns.  They are traditional in northern Spain and especially popular in the Basque country, Navarre, La Rioja, Cantabria, and Asturias. Pinchos are usually eaten in the company of friends or relatives; thus, they have a strong socializing component, and, in the Basque country and Navarre, they are usually regarded as a cornerstone of local culture and society. They are related to tapas, the main difference being that pinchos are usually 'spiked' with a skewer or toothpick, often to a piece of bread. They are served in individual portions and always ordered and paid for independently from the drinks. It is not impossible, however, for the same item to be called pincho in one place and tapa in another."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-pincho"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pincho"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Basque cuisine","cuisine/basque-cuisine"],["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Spain on a Fork","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spainonafork.com","note":"Albert Bevia's video channel and site of traditional and everyday Spanish recipes.","url":"https://spainonafork.com/welcome2/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"More from Basque cuisine","links":[["Basque cheesecake","dish/basque-cheesecake"],["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"],["Gilda (snack)","dish/gilda-snack"],["Goxua","dish/goxua"],["Intxaursaltsa","dish/intxaursaltsa"],["Pantxineta","dish/pantxineta"],["Piperade","dish/piperade"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"],["Sukalki","dish/sukalki"],["Tuna pot","dish/tuna-pot"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/5/56/Pinchos_bermeo_2.JPG/500px-Pinchos_bermeo_2.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"I, Telle","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pinchos_bermeo_2.JPG"}},"dish/vegetable-soup":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/vegetable-soup","n":"Vegetable soup","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 3 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Vegetable soup is a common soup prepared using vegetables (including leaf vegetables, and sometimes loosely mushrooms) as primary ingredients. It dates to ancient history, and in modern times is also a mass-produced food product."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-vegetable-soup"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vegetable_soup"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gastronomy-wine/gastronomy/kanak-cooking","suggested":true,"kind":"site"},{"name":"Olga's Flavor Factory","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"olgasflavorfactory.com","note":"Russian-born home cook Olga's blog, with a dedicated Russian recipes category.","url":"https://www.olgasflavorfactory.com/category/russianrecipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["Adventureland Bazaar","dish/adventureland-bazaar"],["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Bayonne ham","dish/bayonne-ham"],["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Beurre maître d'hôtel","dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish/beurre-manie"],["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Blanquette de veau","dish/blanquette-de-veau"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bouillabaisse","dish/bouillabaisse"],["Bourride","dish/bourride"],["Brioche","dish/brioche"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Calisson","dish/calisson"],["Canapé","dish/canape"],["Cervelle de canut","dish/cervelle-de-canut"],["Cream of sorrel soup","dish/cream-of-sorrel-soup"],["Crêpes Suzette","dish/crepes-suzette"],["Croquembouche","dish/croquembouche"],["Crozets de Savoie","dish/crozets-de-savoie"],["Crudités","dish/crudites"],["Damien Pignolet","dish/damien-pignolet"],["Daube","dish/daube"],["Demi-glace","dish/demi-glace"],["Duxelles","dish/duxelles"],["Egg wash","dish/egg-wash"],["Espagnole sauce","dish/espagnole-sauce"],["Fin gras du Mézenc","dish/fin-gras-du-mezenc"],["Flammekueche","dish/flammekueche"],["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"],["Foin de Crau","dish/foin-de-crau"],["Fraisier","dish/fraisier"],["French onion soup","dish/french-onion-soup"],["French restaurant","dish/french-restaurant"],["Fricassee","dish/fricassee"],["Garbure","dish/garbure"],["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/a/a2/Tom_jued_tahoo.jpg/500px-Tom_jued_tahoo.jpg","w":500,"h":335,"by":"Takeaway","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tom_jued_tahoo.jpg"}},"dish/cochinita-pibil":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/cochinita-pibil","n":"Cochinita pibil","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Maya cuisine","dot":"#94bd5c","intro":[["Pork marinated in ",["achiote","ingredient/annatto"]," and sour orange, wrapped in banana leaves and slow cooked, traditionally underground in a pib. The ",["Maya","cuisine/maya-cuisine"]," cooked birds and deer this way and took up pork soon after the Spanish brought pigs. It's served shredded with warm tortillas, pickled red onion and a little habanero."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Mexico News Daily, Taste of Mexico, cochinita pibil","url":"https://mexiconewsdaily.com/food/taste-of-mexico-cochinita-pibil/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cochinita-pibil"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cochinita_pibil"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Maya cuisine","cuisine/maya-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Achiote","ingredient/annatto"],["Banana leaf","ingredient/banana-leaf"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Seville orange","ingredient/seville-orange"]]},{"h":"More from Maya cuisine","links":[["Kak'ik","dish/kak-ik"],["Pib","dish/piib"],["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/8/84/Cochinita_pibil_2.jpg/500px-Cochinita_pibil_2.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Popo le Chien","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cochinita_pibil_2.jpg"}},"ingredient/nixtamalization":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/nixtamalization","n":"Nixtamalization","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 0 dishes across 0 cuisines","intro":[["Cooking dried corn with lime or wood ash, which loosens the hulls and makes the corn more nourishing. It's the first step toward ",["Masa","ingredient/masa"],", tortillas and ",["Tejate","dish/tejate"],", and UNESCO counts it as one of the foundations of traditional Mexican cuisine. In ",["Nahuatl","cuisine/nahua-foodways"],", the treated corn dough is nextamalli. Farther north, the same idea gives ",["Hominy","ingredient/hominy"],"."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"UNESCO, Traditional Mexican cuisine, the Michoacán paradigm (decision 5.COM 6.30)","url":"https://ich.unesco.org/en/decisions/5.COM/6.30"},{"name":"Roads & Kingdoms, A hundred ladies stirring chocolate drinks","url":"https://roadsandkingdoms.com/2016/hundred-ladies-stirring-chocolate-drinks/"},{"name":"University of Texas at Austin, Tlahtolli, Our cornfield and our food","url":"https://tlahtolli.coerll.utexas.edu/tlamachtiliztli-20-lesson-20-nomillah-huah-notlacualiztli-our-cornfield-and-our-food/"}],"actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nixtamalization"}],"sections":[{"h":"Mentioned in","links":[["Masa","ingredient/masa"],["Purépecha foodways","cuisine/purepecha-foodways"],["Tejate","dish/tejate"],["Zapotec foodways","cuisine/zapotec-foodways"]]}]}}